      "writtenOn": "2025-11-19T07:38:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure I understand why we need this class.\n\nThe purpose of TraceTimer is to record a performance log entry and to add debug logs for the start and end of the operation.\n\nThe metric times report the execution to Gerrit\u0027s MetricMaker, but additionally also already record a performance log entry and add the same debug logs for the start and end of the operation.\n\nWhat is CombinedTraceAndMetricTimer doing additionally?",
      "revId": "036d56f94a486911687b95504dc368bb118ed801",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0bed144b_aeb6804d",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 11,
      "author": {
        "id": 1146706
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-21T11:36:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e The metric times report the execution to Gerrit\u0027s MetricMaker, but additionally also already record a performance log entry and add the same debug logs for the start and end of the operation.\n\nCan you please point me to this code? I cannot find it. And this is the exact purpose of this class.",
      "parentUuid": "886faf5b_65e32d24",
      "revId": "036d56f94a486911687b95504dc368bb118ed801",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "93b809fb_a4a057a0",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 11,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003935
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-21T12:46:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sure, this is in all the Timer classes (Timer0 [1], Timer1 [2], Timer2 [3] and Timer3 [4]).\n\nFor example for Timer0:\n- writing of the start log is at [5]\n- writing of the end log and the performance log entry is at [6]\n\nNote that there are 2 ways how Timer metrics can be used:\n\n1. By calling start [7] and then auto-closing the context that was returned\n2. By calling record [8] and providing the duration\n\nIn case of 2. no start log is written, since the timer is only invoked after the operation is already done. We should use method 2. only in exception cases though.\n\n[1] https://gerrit.googlesource.com/gerrit/+/master/java/com/google/gerrit/metrics/Timer0.java\n\n[2] https://gerrit.googlesource.com/gerrit/+/master/java/com/google/gerrit/metrics/Timer1.java\n\n[3] https://gerrit.googlesource.com/gerrit/+/master/java/com/google/gerrit/metrics/Timer2.java\n\n[4] https://gerrit.googlesource.com/gerrit/+/master/java/com/google/gerrit/metrics/Timer3.java\n\n[5] https://gerrit.googlesource.com/gerrit/+/master/java/com/google/gerrit/metrics/Timer0.java#71\n\n[6] https://gerrit.googlesource.com/gerrit/+/master/java/com/google/gerrit/metrics/Timer0.java#104\n\n[7] https://gerrit.googlesource.com/gerrit/+/master/java/com/google/gerrit/metrics/Timer0.java#68",
      "parentUuid": "0bed144b_aeb6804d",
      "revId": "036d56f94a486911687b95504dc368bb118ed801",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "516a3b20_b664896c",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
